  "beats": [
    {
      "beat_id": "B00",
      "act": "ASK",
      "narration_text": "Hallo. I'm Liam, in for Kumar Karthik. Measure the same SERS substrate in three spots and you can get three different spectra. Why? Because the signal doesn't come from where you think. Here's the short version.",
      "new_visual_element": "Composer: why do spectra vary?",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "greeting": "Hallo, Liam",
            "topic": "SERS · PHYSICS",
            "segment": "Hot Spots",
            "command": "/explain why do my SERS spectra vary spot to spot?",
            "runningText": "mapping the enhancement…",
            "folderLabel": "@KumarKarthik",
            "output": [
              "1. Enhancement isn't uniform",
              "2. It piles up in nanometre gaps",
              "3. A few sites dominate the signal"
            ]
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B00.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-19T02:20:28.937988+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B00.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 10.86,
      "render_duration_s": 10.875,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B00.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ClaudeComposerAsk",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "BLUF",
      "narration_text": "Here's the common picture: SERS signal comes from the whole surface. It doesn't. Most of it comes from a tiny number of hot spots.",
      "new_visual_element": "everywhere → hot spots",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "BrutalistHesitantWriter",
          "props": {
            "text": "SERS\nsignal\ncomes from\neverywhere.",
            "triggerWords": "everywhere",
            "replacementWords": "hot spots",
            "face": "mono",
            "fontSize": 225,
            "lineSpacing": 0.95,
            "seed": "sers-hotspots-bluf",
            "lead_silence_s": 0.4,
            "mistakeRate": 3,
            "hesitateWithin": 1,
            "hesitateBetween": 5,
            "charMs": 30
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-19T02:21:09.743852+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.19,
      "render_duration_s": 7.208333333333333,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:BrutalistHesitantWriter",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "FOURTH_POWER",
      "narration_text": "Start with the physics. SERS enhancement scales roughly with the fourth power of the local electric field. That turns small field boosts into enormous signal boosts. Double the field, and the signal goes up about sixteen times. Make it ten times stronger, and the signal goes up about ten thousand.",
      "new_visual_element": "|E|⁴ bars",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "signal ∝ |E|⁴"
          },
          {
            "at": 2.3,
            "event": "field ×2 → signal ×16"
          },
          {
            "at": 6.7,
            "event": "field ×10 → signal ≈ ×10,000"
          },
          {
            "at": 12.1,
            "event": "log-scale tag; kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B02_FourthPower",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "The fourth-power rule",
          "mechanic": "Log-scale bars: each signal bar is 4× its field bar",
          "note": "|E|⁴ is the standard approximation for electromagnetic SERS enhancement (cited in the paper via Dewanjee et al. 2026). 2⁴=16, 10⁴=10,000 are arithmetic."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.38,
      "render_duration_s": 15.416666666666666,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "GAP",
      "narration_text": "Where does the field get that strong? Mostly in nanometre-scale gaps between metal particles, where light concentrates into a tiny volume. Those gaps are the hot spots. Everywhere else on the surface, the enhancement is far weaker.",
      "new_visual_element": "Nanogap hot spot",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "two nanoparticles"
          },
          {
            "at": 3.4,
            "event": "gap glows"
          },
          {
            "at": 4.8,
            "event": "'nanometre-scale gap: the hot spot'"
          },
          {
            "at": 7.2,
            "event": "'everywhere else: far weaker'"
          },
          {
            "at": 9.5,
            "event": "kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B03_GapHotSpot",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "Where the field piles up",
          "mechanic": "Two discs with a glowing gap",
          "note": "SCHEMATIC, not to scale; labeled on screen."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 12.57,
      "render_duration_s": 12.583333333333334,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "SIXTY_THREE",
      "narration_text": "How lopsided is it? In a classic 2008 study, Fang, Seong, and Dlott measured silver-coated nanospheres. The hottest sites held just sixty-three molecules in a million, but produced twenty-four percent of the total signal. Almost none of the molecules. A quarter of the light.",
      "new_visual_element": "63 per million → 24%",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "two 100% tracks"
          },
          {
            "at": 3.1,
            "event": "molecules: 0.0063%"
          },
          {
            "at": 6.4,
            "event": "signal: 24%"
          },
          {
            "at": 10.1,
            "event": "source line; kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B04_SixtyThree",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "63 in a million",
          "mechanic": "Near-invisible bar vs quarter-length bar on matching tracks",
          "note": "Fang, Seong & Dlott, Science 321 (2008): hottest sites = 63 per million molecules, 24% of SERS intensity; benzenethiolate on 330 nm Ag-coated nanospheres, 532 nm."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.81,
      "render_duration_s": 14.833333333333334,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "WHY_ML",
      "narration_text": "That's why it matters for machine learning. Each measurement spot samples a different set of hot spots, so spectra from one substrate can vary a lot. Split those spectra across training and test, and your model can learn the substrate instead of the analyte. Keep spots from one sample together, and test on new substrate batches.",
      "new_visual_element": "Spot-to-spot variation",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "one substrate with hot spots"
          },
          {
            "at": 3.0,
            "event": "three measurement spots"
          },
          {
            "at": 5.0,
            "event": "three different spectra"
          },
          {
            "at": 7.6,
            "event": "caption: same substrate, different spectra"
          },
          {
            "at": 11.1,
            "event": "caption: split by sample; kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B05_WhyModelCares",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "Why your model cares",
          "mechanic": "Three rings on a dotted strip map to three differently scaled spectra",
          "note": "ILLUSTRATIVE spectra and hot-spot positions. ML advice mirrors the paper's §2.6 and §3.4."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.98,
      "render_duration_s": 17.0,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "VERDICT",
      "narration_text": "Here's the verdict. SERS signal is concentrated, not spread out. A field boost becomes a fourth-power signal boost, it piles up in nanogaps, and a tiny fraction of sites can carry a large share of the signal. That's the physical root of SERS variability, and the reason careful validation matters.",
      "new_visual_element": "Verdict card",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeVerdictArtifact",
          "props": {
            "artifactTitle": "Hot Spots · Summary",
            "artifactHeading": "Most signal, almost no sites",
            "artifactLines": [
              "Enhancement scales roughly with the fourth power of the local field.",
              "Field ×10 means signal of roughly ×10,000.",
              "The field concentrates in nanometre-scale gaps: hot spots.",
              "Fang et al. 2008: 63 sites per million gave 24% of the signal.",
              "Spot-to-spot variation follows directly from this.",
              "For ML: split by sample, and test on new substrate batches."
            ]
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B06.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-19T02:22:24.299857+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.72,
      "render_duration_s": 15.75,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:ClaudeVerdictArtifact",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:28:23"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "act": "HANDOFF",
      "narration_text": "Your turn. Look at how you split your data. Do all the spectra from one substrate land on the same side? If not, start there.",
